# Retrieves the top 10 payment executors by total awarded value (converted to EUR) for a date range.
# Returns: payment executor name and the total awarded value in EUR, ordered by highest value.
# Defaults to the last 7 days excluding today; change the range below to query a different period.
#
# This query's structure (grain, joins, winning-tender rule, currency table, name
# normalisation) is mirrored in queries/top-budget-providers-by-awarded-value.sparql.
# If you change any of that logic here, apply the same change there, and re-verify both
# against the live endpoint - the two are hand-duplicated, not generated from one
# source, and have drifted out of sync before.
#
# Grain: payment executors are a LOT-RESULT-level role (mapped from the eForms Payer
# Party on each efac:LotResult), and different lot results within one notice can have
# different payment executors. So this query works at the lot-result level, NOT the
# notice level: the value used is the winning tender's financial offer value for that
# lot result (LotAwardOutcome -> TenderAwardOutcome -> Tender -> financial offer value),
# and that per-lot-result value is split evenly across the payment executors of the SAME
# lot result (amount / number of payment executors on that lot result). This avoids
# attributing a notice-level total (epo:hasTotalAwardedValue) to lot-level roles.
#
# Join: the notice is linked to its lot result explicitly through the shared Lot
# (notice epo:refersToLot ?lot; lot award outcome epo:concernsLot ?lot), not through
# named-graph co-occurrence. The payment-executor count is computed in its own scoped
# subquery ahead of the tender-value joins; nesting it inside the main join pattern is
# much slower and can time out.
#
# Winning tender only: a lot can have several lot award outcomes, one per tenderer that
# competed for it (not just the winner) - verified against real notices, both as several
# tender award outcomes under ONE lot award outcome, and as several lot award outcomes
# each with one tenderer, all concerning the SAME lot. Summing every tender's value would
# count non-winning tenders' values too. To keep only the winning tender(s), per LOT (not
# per lot award outcome):
#   - if any tender on the lot has epo:hasAwardRank 1, keep all such tenders (a lot can
#     legitimately have more than one winner, e.g. a multi-award framework - verified
#     against a real notice with dozens of rank-1 tenders at distinct amounts);
#   - otherwise, if ranking was not used on that lot at all (no tender has any rank) and
#     there is exactly one tender on the lot, keep that one;
#   - otherwise (ranked with no rank-1 tender, e.g. the winner's rank was withheld for
#     privacy, or unranked with more than one tender) the lot is genuinely ambiguous and
#     is excluded rather than guessed at.
#
# Coverage: the payment executor (eForms Payer Party) is an OPTIONAL field. Measured
# against a sample week (2026-07-01 to 2026-07-07): about 814 of 30,037 lot award
# outcomes (roughly 3%) declared a payment executor. This query only covers that small
# subset, so it is a "top payment executors among those declared", not a ranking over
# all awarded value in the date range. The exact share will vary by period.
#
# Currency conversion: the TED Open Data Service stores financial values only in their
# original currency (see the known issues and limitations page -
# https://docs.ted.europa.eu/ODS/latest/querying/known_issues.html), so this query
# converts to EUR using the inline exchange-rate table below (rates as of June 2026).
# These rates are approximate and go stale over time; update them as needed. Amounts in
# a currency that is not present in the table are EXCLUDED from the totals (a payment
# executor's total may therefore be understated if some of its awards were in an
# unlisted currency).
#
# Outliers: some notices carry an implausibly large hasFinancialOfferValue for their
# winning tender - for example, notice 00840791-2025 has a winning tender value of
# 260,000,000,000 (EUR). This is a data quality issue in the notice itself, not a query
# error, and such notices can dominate the top 10.
#
# Duplicate names: the same real organisation can be recorded with a different name
# across notices - a leading/trailing space or a difference in capitalisation are the
# most common cases. To avoid splitting one organisation's total across two rows for
# just these two cases, the query groups by a normalised form of the name
# (?paymentExecutorKey: outer spaces stripped, upper-cased) rather than the name as-is,
# and displays one of the original, differently-spelled variants (picked arbitrarily via
# SAMPLE) as ?paymentExecutorName. This does not catch other kinds of name variation -
# internal spacing, punctuation, abbreviations, translations. For example, this data
# also has at least a dozen further spellings of one Spanish rail body alone (hyphen vs
# space vs comma vs dash as separator, an "Órgano de Contratación de ..." prefix on
# some), which this normalisation does not merge. Check the results for further
# near-duplicates before treating the ranking as final.
#
# Performance: most of the cost is in the winning-tender subquery, which is not scoped
# to the date range (see the note above it) - so runtime does not scale much with the
# size of the range. Timing is also unstable: three consecutive runs of the exact same
# single-day query took 28, 35 and 51 seconds; a run over 2025-01-01 to 2025-12-31 (1
# year) took about 26 seconds. A 2-year range (2024-01-01 to 2025-12-31) was also tried
# but the connection was dropped after several minutes without a result, so that range
# is not recommended. A short range is not reliably fast, and one successful timing is
# not a guarantee of similar timing on a later run. Allow at least a couple of minutes
# and expect variation.

PREFIX epo: <http://data.europa.eu/a4g/ontology#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/>

SELECT (SAMPLE(?rawPaymentExecutorName) AS ?paymentExecutorName) (SUM(?amountEURShare) AS ?totalAwardedEUR)
WHERE {
  # Number of distinct payment executor organisations on each lot result in range, so
  # that lot result's awarded value can be split evenly across its payment executors
  # instead of being counted in full for each one. Kept as a standalone subquery,
  # scoped to the same date range, and evaluated ahead of the tender-value joins below.
  {
    SELECT ?lotAwardOutcome (COUNT(DISTINCT ?peOrgForCount) AS ?paymentExecutorCount)
    WHERE {
      GRAPH ?g {
        ?notice a epo:ResultNotice ;
                epo:hasPublicationDate ?pubDate ;
                epo:refersToLot ?lot .
        # Default range: the last 7 days, excluding today (7 days ago through yesterday).
        BIND (xsd:date(NOW()) - "P7D"^^xsd:duration AS ?publicationStartDate)
        BIND (xsd:date(NOW()) - "P1D"^^xsd:duration AS ?publicationEndDate)
        # To use a fixed range instead, comment out the two BINDs above and uncomment the line below:
        # VALUES (?publicationStartDate ?publicationEndDate) { ("2025-01-01"^^xsd:date "2025-01-07"^^xsd:date) }
        FILTER(?pubDate >= ?publicationStartDate && ?pubDate <= ?publicationEndDate)

        # Explicit join from the notice to the lot result through the shared Lot.
        ?lotAwardOutcome a epo:LotAwardOutcome ;
                         epo:concernsLot ?lot ;
                         rdfs:seeAlso ?peForCount .
        ?peForCount a epo:PaymentExecutor ;
                    epo:playedBy ?peOrgForCount .
      }
    }
    GROUP BY ?lotAwardOutcome
  }

  # The winning tender award outcome(s) per lot (see "Winning tender only" above): those
  # at hasAwardRank 1 if ranking is used on the lot, otherwise the lot's only tender if
  # ranking is not used at all. Kept as its own subquery so the rule is evaluated once
  # per lot rather than repeated for every payment executor on it.
  #
  # Note: this subquery is not scoped to the date range above - it is joined to the
  # rest of the query via ?lot and ?tenderAwardOutcome instead. Adding an explicit
  # date/notice filter here was tried (on the equivalent budget-providers query) and
  # made the query slower, not faster (likely a join-order change), so it is left
  # unscoped despite computing over more lots than strictly needed for a short date
  # range.
  {
    SELECT ?lot ?tenderAwardOutcome WHERE {
      GRAPH ?g3 {
        ?anyLao epo:concernsLot ?lot ; epo:comprisesTenderAwardOutcome ?tenderAwardOutcome .
        {
          SELECT ?lot (COUNT(DISTINCT ?t) AS ?tendersOnLot) (COUNT(DISTINCT ?rankedTender) AS ?rankedTendersOnLot) WHERE {
            GRAPH ?g4 {
              ?laoForCount epo:concernsLot ?lot ; epo:comprisesTenderAwardOutcome ?t .
              OPTIONAL {
                ?t epo:hasAwardRank ?anyRank .
                BIND(?t AS ?rankedTender)
              }
            }
          }
          GROUP BY ?lot
        }
        OPTIONAL { ?tenderAwardOutcome epo:hasAwardRank ?rank . }
        FILTER(
          (BOUND(?rank) && ?rank = 1)
          ||
          (?rankedTendersOnLot = 0 && ?tendersOnLot = 1)
        )
      }
    }
  }

  # Payment executors for the same lot result as the winning tender award outcome(s)
  # above, and that tender's financial offer value.
  GRAPH ?g2 {
    ?lotAwardOutcome epo:concernsLot ?lot ;
                     rdfs:seeAlso ?paymentExecutor ;
                     epo:comprisesTenderAwardOutcome ?tenderAwardOutcome .
    ?tenderAwardOutcome epo:concernsTender ?tender .
    ?tender epo:hasFinancialOfferValue ?value .
    ?value epo:hasAmountValue ?amount ;
           epo:hasCurrency ?currencyURI .

    ?paymentExecutor a epo:PaymentExecutor ;
                     epo:playedBy ?organization .
    ?organization epo:hasLegalName ?rawPaymentExecutorName .
  }

  # Normalise the name for grouping only (see "Duplicate names" above); the displayed
  # name keeps its original spelling. SPARQL 1.1's TRIM() is not supported here, so
  # leading/trailing spaces are stripped with REPLACE instead.
  BIND(UCASE(REPLACE(?rawPaymentExecutorName, "^[ ]+|[ ]+$", "")) AS ?paymentExecutorKey)

  # Exchange rate lookup table (rates to EUR) as of June 2026
  VALUES (?currency ?rate) {
    ("EUR" 1.0)
    ("AED" 0.251)
    ("ALL" 0.0098)
    ("AMD" 0.0024)
    ("ANG" 0.513)
    ("AUD" 0.60)
    ("BAM" 0.511)
    ("BGN" 0.511)
    ("BRL" 0.163)
    ("CAD" 0.68)
    ("CHF" 1.05)
    ("CNY" 0.127)
    ("CZK" 0.040)
    ("DKK" 0.134)
    ("EGP" 0.019)
    ("GBP" 1.17)
    ("HKD" 0.118)
    ("HUF" 0.0025)
    ("INR" 0.011)
    ("ISK" 0.0067)
    ("JPY" 0.0062)
    ("LSL" 0.051)
    ("MAD" 0.092)
    ("MDL" 0.052)
    ("MGA" 0.0002)
    ("MKD" 0.016)
    ("MYR" 0.21)
    ("NOK" 0.085)
    ("PLN" 0.233)
    ("RON" 0.201)
    ("RSD" 0.0085)
    ("RWF" 0.0007)
    ("SEK" 0.088)
    ("THB" 0.027)
    ("TND" 0.30)
    ("TRY" 0.027)
    ("TZS" 0.00036)
    ("UAH" 0.022)
    ("USD" 0.92)
    ("USN" 0.92)
    ("UZS" 0.000073)
    ("XAF" 0.00152)
    ("XOF" 0.00152)
  }
  ?currencyURI dc:identifier ?currency .

  # Guard against lot results with a value but no resolvable payment executor count,
  # which would otherwise cause a division-by-zero error.
  FILTER (BOUND(?amount) && BOUND(?paymentExecutorCount) && ?paymentExecutorCount > 0)

  BIND(?amount * ?rate / ?paymentExecutorCount AS ?amountEURShare)
}
GROUP BY ?paymentExecutorKey
ORDER BY DESC(?totalAwardedEUR)
LIMIT 10
